d3d12: scan out through a winsys displaytarget

Against mesa-26.2.0 (9f0a761020bca92f2b07156a0621e5360cb8eca5), the commit
payload.spec.json pins. Not submitted upstream.

A GPU-PV guest has exactly one DRM device, `vmlord_drm`, and it has no render
half -- so gbm loads the kms_swrast screen and `GALLIUM_DRIVER` picks what draws
into it. d3d12 draws there today: a context comes up on the host's adapter and
`eglSwapBuffers` succeeds. What did not work is the last hop, handing the frame
to KMS, and that is the whole of what this patch changes.

Two things were in the way.

The frame was never in a buffer KMS could address. gbm asks for a scanout buffer
and then asks that buffer for a DRM handle; d3d12 returned a resource in D3D12
memory, so the handle came back 0 and `drmModeAddFB2` answered EINVAL. llvmpipe
on the same device answers with handle 1, because it routes a `PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT`
resource onto a winsys displaytarget -- a dumb BO on the KMS device, which does
carry a handle. This patch does the same, and copies the rendered frame into that
displaytarget on `flush_resource`, which the frontend calls before every swap.
Both halves already existed in d3d12: displaytarget allocation through the winsys
and the copy in `d3d12_flush_frontbuffer`. Only `PIPE_BIND_DISPLAY_TARGET` reached
them, and gbm asks for `PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT`.

And with `GBM_BO_USE_SCANOUT` the allocation failed outright, before any of that:
`PIPE_BIND_SCANOUT` forced `D3D12_TEXTURE_LAYOUT_ROW_MAJOR`, which D3D12 refuses
for a committed texture. A resource presented by copying does not need a row-major
layout -- the copy is what makes the layout linear -- so the demand is dropped
whenever there is a winsys to copy into. Without one, nothing here changes.

The displaytarget mapping is held for the life of the resource rather than taken
per present, which is where this hop's cost was. `kms_sw_displaytarget_unmap`
munmaps as soon as its refcount reaches zero, so a map around every present means
mmap'ing the scanout buffer afresh every frame, and the copy then faults in every
page of it before it can write. Measured on an RTX 5070 Ti through GPU-PV, the
same copy costs 1.02/2.49/11.77 ms at 720p/1080p/2160p against a mapping made per
present and 0.07/0.22/2.51 ms against one that is held -- 3.22 GiB/s against
35.95 GiB/s at 1080p. `d3d12_resource_destroy` drops the mapping, because
`kms_sw_displaytarget_destroy` will not munmap a buffer that is still mapped; it
only says so in its debug output.

The cost that remains is one full-frame readback per presented frame: a fixed
cost in exchange for a variable one. Measured at 1080p, drawing N blended
full-screen passes, d3d12 against llvmpipe: 7.75/4.19 ms at N=0, 7.48/8.58 at
N=2, 8.44/28.40 at N=8, 8.18/56.92 at N=16 -- taken before the mapping was held,
so d3d12's flat line is now lower, but it is still flat and llvmpipe's is not.
The two cross at about two passes, which a desktop exceeds before it has drawn a
window.

A frame reaches `flush_resource` twice -- once before the swap from
`notify_before_flush_cb`, and again from `dri2_allocate_textures` when the
drawable is revalidated after it -- and on this path both calls have the same
source and the same destination, with nothing drawn in between. So the copy is
made idempotent rather than performed twice. `dt_presented` says the
displaytarget already holds what the resource holds: the copy sets it, and
everything that writes the resource clears it. There are two such places, and
between them they are every writer. A GPU write is recorded by
`d3d12_batch_reference_resource`, which draws reach through their render targets,
images and streamout buffers and which blits, copies, clears and query writeback
call directly. A CPU write goes through `d3d12_transfer_map`, the one function
behind both `buffer_map` and `texture_map`; a texture cannot take the direct
mapping that would bypass the staging copy, because `can_map_directly` admits
buffers alone. `d3d12_flush_resource` reads the flag before its own batch
reference, which is a write and would otherwise clear it on every call.

Measured on an RTX 5070 Ti through GPU-PV, two Mesa trees differing only in this
and driven by the same probe over 100 frames with no draw load: 1.109/1.854/6.754
ms per frame at 720p/1080p/2160p without the flag and 0.586/0.994/3.399 with it,
1.89x/1.87x/1.99x. The probe's 103 swaps produced 205 presents before and 103
after -- one per swap. It ends by clearing to two colours in turn and reading
each back through `gbm_bo_map`, so a tree that got faster by not presenting fails
rather than scores; both trees pass.

The GPU copy into a `D3D12_HEAP_TYPE_READBACK` staging buffer and the fence wait
on it are what is left, 0.68 ms per present at 1080p. Aiming the GPU copy at the
dumb BO's own pages instead would remove the CPU from this hop entirely, and the
kernel half of that exists -- `dxgkrnl` implements `EXISTINGHEAP` over pinned
guest pages -- but the userspace half does not: the D3D12 runtime a guest gets
from the host's WSL package answers `E_NOTIMPL` to both
`ID3D12Device3::OpenExistingHeapFromAddress` and
`ID3D12Device13::OpenExistingHeapFromAddress1`.

On at least one Intel iGPU this hop is pathological -- WSLg issue #1498 reports
327-359 ms per frame at 720p -- so a host that is not known good deserves a
measurement before this path is trusted.

Refs: #180, #188, #189, #190
